--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0336 revived Flag 5 (semantic isolation), which had been returning
`skipped` on every run. The fix merges with the flag held inert via
`--skip-semantic-flag` in `dvc.yaml`'s `extend.cmd`, because activating it as
shipped would remove 361 works on a threshold no human has ever seen produce
output.

`sigma: 2` was set on a smaller corpus under MiniLM. The embedding model was
swapped to BGE-M3 **15 days after the flag silently died**, so the threshold
deciding 361 removals has never been calibrated against anything it produced.

## What the shipped setting would remove

361 net new removals, reproduced independently of the implementing agent.
Nothing precious by citation: **zero works cited >= 50**, max 43, median 0 — the
core subset (~2,300 papers cited >= 50) loses nothing. Most are genuine harvest
false positives (doctoral viva procedures, Ce-Zr nickel catalysis, fractional
differential equations, mycotoxins in animal feed, Irish negative equity).

But 45 of the 361 titles carry climate vocabulary, and these are in scope:

- *From small to insignificant: Climate impact of the Kyoto Protocol with and
  without the US* (2001, 39 cites)
- *International Carbon Finance and EcoSecurities* (2008)
- *Official Documents — Agreement with France for Donation to the Adaptation
  Fund* (2013)
- *The feasibility of ambitious climate agreements: Norway as an early test
  case* (2002)
- *COP27 climate change conference: urgent action needed for Africa and the
  world* (2022)

A paper titled *International Carbon Finance* culled as a semantic outlier is a
calibration failure, not a filter working.

## The language gradient — the real defect

Baseline removal rate 1.09% of 33,057 candidates. By language, shipped setting:

| lang | candidates | removed | rate | vs baseline |
|------|-----------|---------|------|-------------|
| es | 449 | 20 | 4.45% | 4.1x |
| ko | 87 | 3 | 3.45% | 3.2x |
| pt | 608 | 20 | 3.29% | 3.0x |
| fr | 354 | 10 | 2.82% | 2.6x |
| ru | 119 | 3 | 2.52% | 2.3x |
| en | 30,225 | 288 | 0.95% | 0.9x |

The centroid is built from a 91.6%-English corpus, so distance-from-centroid
partly measures *not-in-English* rather than *off-topic*. Non-English works are
culled at 2.3-4.1x the English rate — and that multilingual tail is the
dependent variable of `docs/research-note-multilingual.md`. Activating the flag
as shipped would thin the population that note studies, while the data paper
reports a six-flag filter whose sixth flag has an unmeasured language gradient.

## Calibration sweep (read-only, frozen corpus)

The per-language arm computes both centroid and mean/std within language,
falling back to the global arm below n=30.

| variant | flagged | net rm | climate-vocab culled | on-topic 6 culled | es | pt | fr | ko | en |
|---------|---------|--------|---------------------|-------------------|----|----|----|----|----|
| global s=2 *(shipped)* | 1276 | 361 | 45 | 6/6 | 4.1x | 3.0x | 2.6x | 3.1x | 0.9x |
| global s=3 | 238 | 38 | 1 | 1/6 | 9.7x | 4.3x | 4.9x | 0x | 0.7x |
| global s=4 | 39 | 5 | 0 | 0/6 | 14.7x | 10.9x | 18.6x | 0x | 0.4x |
| per-lang s=2 | 1324 | 385 | 48 | 6/6 | 1.1x | 0.4x | 1.9x | 1.0x | 1.0x |
| **per-lang s=3** | 238 | **45** | 4 | 1/6 | 0x | 1.2x | 2.1x | 0x | 1.0x |
| per-lang s=4 | 40 | 4 | 1 | 1/6 | 0x | 0x | 23.3x | 0x | 0.8x |

Three findings:

1. **The per-language centroid fixes the gradient at its source.** At s=2 it
   collapses es 4.1x -> 1.1x, pt 3.0x -> 0.4x, ko 3.2x -> 1.0x, en 0.9x -> 1.0x.
2. **Raising sigma against a global centroid makes the bias worse**, not better:
   global s=4 gives es 14.7x, pt 10.9x, fr 18.6x, because the extreme tail of a
   91.6%-English centroid distribution is almost entirely non-English. Sigma is
   the wrong lever for the gradient; the centroid is the right one.
3. **s=4 empties the flag**: 4 removals of 32,938 (0.012%). A sixth flag that
   culls four papers is decorative, and harder to defend in the paper than
   honestly reporting five.

Caveat: at s=3 and s=4 the per-language ratios rest on 0-7 works per language,
so those multipliers are noise (fr 23.3x at s=4 is one paper of 354). They are
not signal.

## Decision (author, 2026-07-27)

**Repurpose Flag 5 as a diagnostic, not a filter** — none of A/B/C below, but
the variant the independent review round recommended. Keep the computation,
correct its geometry, drop the removals:

- `mode: diagnostic` — the distance is computed and published as
  `semantic_outlier_dist`; the flag mask is all-False.
- `centroid: per_language` with `min_language_count: 30` — each work is
  measured against its own language's centroid, falling back to the corpus
  centroid below the floor.
- No `sigma` in config. `mode: filter` still works but demands an explicit,
  calibrated sigma; an unread threshold left in config is a landmine.

Why not A: it filters on a cut no human validation supports. Why not B: it
destroys an isolation signal worth publishing with the deposit, and the
removals it kept would go on thinning the multilingual tail
`docs/research-note-multilingual.md` exists to study. (Correction to the
first draft of this rationale: that note computes its own k-NN isolation
score against EN-North neighbours, §4.1 — it does not read
`semantic_outlier_dist`. What Flag 5 threatened was the note's *population*,
not its statistic. Verified against the note, 2026-07-27.)
The options below are kept as the calibration record they were.

## Options (as put to the author)

- **A. Per-language centroid at s=3** (recommended at the time). 45 removals, flat gradient
  (en 1.0x, pt 1.2x, es 0x), 4 climate-vocab works culled against 45 shipped. A
  flag that filters without a language gradient.
- **B. Retire Flag 5.** Defensible: Flag 6 (LLM relevance) already flags 11,244
  works and does topical filtering better; Flag 5's marginal contribution at s=3
  is 45 works of 33,000. The paper would describe five flags, which is what the
  artifact has actually been for months. Cheapest and most honest option.
- **C. Per-language centroid at s=2.** Fixes the gradient but still culls all six
  named on-topic works. Not recommended.

Under every variant but global s=4 the *Adaptation Fund* document is culled. It
is an `Official Documents` record, so that is an abstract-quality problem, not a
threshold problem — no sigma fixes it. Handle separately or accept.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/filter_flags.py` — `flag_semantic_outlier` (centroid and threshold)
- `scripts/harvest/corpus_filter.py` — `load_embeddings`, `_flag5_subset`
- `config/corpus_filter.yaml` — `semantic_outlier.sigma`, `min_coverage`
- `dvc.yaml` — `extend.cmd` carries `--skip-semantic-flag`; removing it activates
- `docs/research-note-multilingual.md` — the note whose signal Flag 5 removes on
- `deliverables/data-paper/data-paper.qmd` and the shared includes — described
  six removal flags while the artifact has five plus a diagnostic column
- follow-up: 0401, the Flag 6 concept-group gate this decision leaves exposed

## Actions

1. ~~Author decides A, B, or C.~~ Decided above: the diagnostic variant.
2. `config/corpus_filter.yaml` — drop `sigma`, add `mode: diagnostic`,
   `centroid: per_language`, `min_language_count: 30`; keep `min_coverage`.
3. `scripts/filter_flags.py::flag_semantic_outlier` — honour both keys.
   Diagnostic mode returns an all-False mask *and* the real distances; the
   per-language arm computes centroid and moments within language, falling
   back to the global arm below the floor. `mode: filter` keeps working with
   an explicit sigma, and is not the default.
4. `dvc.yaml` — drop `--skip-semantic-flag` from `extend.cmd` (the flag is
   inert by config now, and the CLI switch also killed the computation).
   Keep `data/catalogs/embeddings.npz` in `extend.deps`: a diagnostic column
   goes stale as silently as a filtering one.
5. Prose: five removal flags plus one diagnostic column. Correct the data
   paper (§2.2 twice), the corpus report, `corpus-filtering.md`,
   `corpus-enrichment.md`, and the deposit contract in
   `scripts/_deposit_variables.py`; regenerate `codebook.md` and
   `tab_variables.md` from the generator rather than editing them.

## Test

- A test asserting the language gradient stays inside a stated band after
  calibration. The defect this ticket exists for is invisible to any count
  assertion; only a per-language rate comparison sees it. It needs a control
  arm too: without one, the band test keeps passing on a fixture that has
  lost the gradient.
- Diagnostic mode yields an all-False mask *and* non-null, varying distances —
  the pairing is what stops the mode degenerating into a stub.
- The per-language centroid is actually used, and the small-language fallback
  triggers below the floor.
- `mode: filter` still filters, and refuses to run without a sigma.

## Verification

- [x] Diagnostic variant implemented, language gradient measured afterwards
      (global es 14.3% / ru 15.5% / pt 9.2% / fr 7.9% vs en 3.4%;
      per-language 3.8% / 7.3% / 3.6% / 4.5% vs en 4.0%)
- [x] `refined_works.csv` unchanged, and provably so: the flag mask is
      all-False on the frozen corpus (0 of 32,938 candidates), so the filter
      stage sees no Flag 5 removal at all
- [x] Distances still emitted: 32,938 non-null, min 0.1443 / median 0.2776 /
      max 0.5946; per-language centroid for 32,719 in 16 languages, corpus
      centroid for the remaining 219
- [x] Data paper and the shared includes describe five removal flags plus a
      diagnostic column
- [x] `--skip-semantic-flag` removed from `dvc.yaml` — safe because the flag
      is inert by config, so this activates a computation, not a deletion

## Invariants

- The corpus is frozen (author, 2026-07-27) mid RDJ-26561 R&R. Nothing under
  `data/` is written; the measurements above are read-only recomputations.
- No weakening of acceptance thresholds.
- 0362's checkpoint concern does not gate this change: diagnostic mode removes
  nothing, so a rebuild cannot move a downstream count through Flag 5. It
  still gates any future `mode: filter` activation.

## Exit criteria

- Flag 5 computes and publishes a per-language semantic distance and removes
  no work; the removal path survives behind `mode: filter` with an explicit
  sigma, and is not the default.
- The measured language gradient is recorded, and the corpus is untouched.
- The paper describes five removal flags plus one diagnostic column.
